    {
        "content": "<p>I've been working on formalizing some basic theorems in infinitary model theory, especially the countable case Lω₁ω, and would appreciate feedback on which API shape would likely make the most sense for mathlib, before I try to turn some of this into PRs.</p>\n<p>I currently have a <a href=\"https://cameronfreer.github.io/infinitary-logic/docs/InfinitaryLogic/Linf/Syntax.html\">working syntax layer</a> that extends <code>FirstOrder.Language.BoundedFormula</code> to <code>BoundedFormulaInf</code> via infinitary conjunctions/disjunctions (<code>iInf</code> / <code>iSup</code>, indexed by arbitrary types), together with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>semantics,</li>\n<li><code>relabel</code> / <code>subst</code> / <code>mapLanguage</code>, and</li>\n<li>a countability predicate <code>IsCountable</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On top of that there's also an Lω₁ω convenience layer <code>BoundedFormulaω</code>, with translations <code>toLinf</code> / <code>ofCountable</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would mathlib likely prefer a single core L∞ω syntax, with Lω₁ω handled via countability predicates/conversions?</li>\n<li>There are also some universe-packaging issues for Karp-style elementary equivalence arguments; I have a temporary stopgap for this, but I suspect there's a better solution longer-term.</li>\n<li>Any other feedback on the basics or the results so far (Scott sentences, Karp's theorem, model existence via consistency properties, etc.) would be very welcome.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thanks!</p>\n<p>See also: <a href=\"https://cameronfreer.github.io/infinitary-logic/\">project page</a>;  <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic\">github repo</a>; <a href=\"https://cameronfreer.github.io/infinitary-logic/blueprint/\">blueprint</a></p>",
        "id": 579814102,
        "sender_full_name": "Cameron Freer",
        "timestamp": 1773701728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I've been working more in the <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic\">infinitary-logic</a> repo, and I now have a more concrete proposal for a first PR to mathlib on Lω₁ω and L∞ω syntax, semantics, and embeddings, extending mathlib's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/ModelTheory/Syntax.html#FirstOrder.Language.BoundedFormula\"><code>FirstOrder.Language.BoundedFormula</code></a> API.</p>\n<p>I think these three main choices are appropriate, but are there any reasons I should reconsider? Also, does anyone have naming feedback?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two parallel inductives:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic/blob/1a478e80794080a536931c029281bb65266b3171/InfinitaryLogic/Lomega1omega/Syntax.lean#L43\"><code>BoundedFormulaω</code></a> has <code>ℕ</code>-indexed infinitary nodes, while <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic/blob/1a478e80794080a536931c029281bb65266b3171/InfinitaryLogic/Linf/Syntax.lean#L43\"><code>BoundedFormulaInf</code></a> has nodes indexed by arbitrary types:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iSup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iSup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The alternative would be one <code>BoundedFormulaInf</code> plus a countable-fragment predicate or subtype. However, in my work so far, there are structural traversals throughout results on Scott analysis, polarity, quantifier classes, and interpolation, and I think a fragment subtype would make each such operation carry and preserve a countability proof.</p>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li>\n<p><strong>Both <code>iSup</code> and <code>iInf</code> are primitive:</strong> This way there are direct structural cases for semantics, polarity, and rank; otherwise, if one were defined in terms of the negation of the other, I think proofs would have to unfold the encoding and do more work, but maybe there's a more elegant solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>L∞ω supports arbitrary index universes:</strong>  Karp’s standard back-and-forth argument forms conjunctions indexed by the carrier of an arbitrary structure (<a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic/blob/1a478e80794080a536931c029281bb65266b3171/InfinitaryLogic/Karp/Theorem.lean#L355-L358\">source</a>):</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">iInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ψ</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here <code>N : Type w</code>, so restricting infinitary indices to <code>Type 0</code> is insufficient.</p>\n<p>As an API consequence, I propose making the Lω₁ω → L∞ω embedding polymorphic in its target index universe:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω.toLinf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">L.BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">L.BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u'</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I've tested this through semantics and have regression examples at nonzero index universes.</p>\n<p>My proposed first PR would contain only the following (operations, language maps, rank, fragment predicates, and theories could come later):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the two syntaxes and their derived connectives;</li>\n<li>semantics and constructor simp lemmas;</li>\n<li>first-order → Lω₁ω;</li>\n<li>first-order → L∞ω;</li>\n<li>Lω₁ω → L∞ω;</li>\n<li>realization preservation and the commuting embedding triangle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Does anyone have any suggestions? Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 613374571,
        "sender_full_name": "Cameron Freer",
        "timestamp": 1785314614
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's the <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic/compare/1a478e8...api/linf-target-universe-embeddings\">prototype</a> where I've been testing the ingredients towards the first mathlib PR. It includes the universe-polymorphic realization API, the target-polymorphic Lω₁ω → L∞ω embedding, nonzero-universe regressions, and the module documentation.</p>",
        "id": 613390367,
        "sender_full_name": "Cameron Freer",
        "timestamp": 1785319728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"116846\">Cameron Freer</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/ModelTheory.3A.20API.20for.20infinitary.20formulas.20of.20L_.7B.E2.88.9E.2C.CF.89.7D/near/613374571\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>while <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic/blob/1a478e80794080a536931c029281bb65266b3171/InfinitaryLogic/Linf/Syntax.lean#L43\"><code>BoundedFormulaInf</code></a> has nodes indexed by arbitrary types:</p>\n<p><div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iSup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iSup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormulaInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div><br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This looks very scary. Instead I would suggest <code>BoundedFormulaInf</code> to factor out an additional type parameter <code>(ι : Type uι)</code> and then only accept indexing by <code>ι</code> in the <code>iSup</code> and <code>iInf</code> constructors. The original behavior can be recovered by passing in <code>(ι := Type uι)</code>, but that shouldn't be necessary.</p>",
        "id": 613390601,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1785319810
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>a nice side effect is that <code>BoundedFormulaω</code> then becomes a special case of <code>BoundedFormulaInf</code> with <code>(ι := ℕ)</code></p>",
        "id": 613390813,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
